issues.recommendation:
Reframe M6 as a feasibility gate with preregistered quantitative progression criteria (green/amber/red):
- admissible-mutant yield per family;
- fraction of mutants that only crash or fail the schema;
- refinement stability (share of reference protocol-feature pairs whose h vs h/2 difference is below the feature floor);
- rerun determinism;
- runtime per model x protocol x dt level;
- false-positive count on valid transformations, with its exact interval;
- a non-trivial drift criterion: silent drift in at least two operator families beyond stimulus bookkeeping, in both models, each case manually audited as scientifically interpretable.

Also:
- Resolve the 10-20 versus 20-60 mutant inconsistency.
- State that pilot models are discovery-only.
- Forbid using pilot detection rates for sample-size calculations; use the pilot only to estimate attrition, runtime and numerical stability.

issues.analysis:
The design creates many comparisons:
- selected versus canonical, count-matched random and runtime-matched random;
- several budgets k (the detection-versus-cost curve invites picking the best-looking k);
- 6-8 per-family breakdowns;
- stricter and looser tolerance variants;
- held-out models versus the held-out family;
- feature and protocol attributions (RQ6).

Success requires 'most of' nine criteria, and the pivot criteria are qualitative ('nearly everything', 'high'). That leaves many analysis paths open after the data are seen, exactly the prediction-versus-postdiction confusion preregistration is meant to prevent (Nosek et al., PNAS 2018). The handoff gives no primary k (only the example command '--budget 4'), no confirmatory set and no error-control strategy. Fixed-sequence, gatekeeping and weighted Bonferroni procedures map the priority of research questions onto a multiple-test procedure, and Bretz et al. (Stat Med) give a graphical way to build and communicate them.

issues.recommendation:
- Preregister one primary budget k, justified from discovery data before any held-out access, and one primary tolerance setting.
- Define a confirmatory fixed-sequence hierarchy, testing each step only if the previous one succeeds:
  H1 selected vs canonical on held-out units;
  H2 selected vs count-matched random;
  H3 selected vs runtime-matched random;
  H4 held-out mutation family.
- Label everything else (other k values, per-family results, tolerance variants, RQ6 attributions) as exploratory, either without p-values or with explicitly unadjusted ones.
- Turn the success and pivot criteria into preregistered quantitative decision rules, stated in plain English in DECISIONS.md.

issues.analysis:
Timing: several steps can expose held-out outcomes before the 'final evaluation' boundary is explicit.
(i) classify-mutants on held-out mutants must run the full exhaustive battery, and since every strategy is a subset of it, classification reveals all strategies' held-out outcomes (STATS-01).
(ii) Inclusion checks on held-out models (deterministic execution, meaningful current response) and per-model tolerance terms need held-out reference runs. That is acceptable only if automated by frozen rules and declared in advance.
(iii) Any curation of held-out mutants after classification.

Content: the handoff's list omits items that decide results:
- cluster definition and estimand weighting (STATS-02);
- whether the battery contains canonical (STATS-04);
- canonical-baseline definition (STATS-06);
- primary k and greedy tie-break (STATS-08, STATS-12);
- random-baseline construction and cost proxy (STATS-07);
- multiplicity hierarchy (STATS-12);
- generator grid and seed (STATS-08);
- which family is held out (STATS-09);
- how defined-to-undefined feature flips and the categorical firing-regime feature (to which no numeric tolerance applies) count as detections;
- rheobase handling for mutants (STATS-14);
- handling of reference models that fail a protocol;
- deviation reporting and environment hashes.
The FINAL_SPEC adds undefined features and the canonical protocol, but not the rest.

Platform: the M0 venue audit verified that OSF registrations are frozen and time-stamped, cannot be edited (only withdrawn), and offer a Simulation Studies template, so the content must be complete when registered. Morris, White and Crowther (Stat Med 2019) give a structured approach to planning computational experiments that fits this design.
